******************************************************************************

4/13/92 MacMach note:

The "Mach Kernel" that is shipped along with the "Mach" control panel will
automatically run the "newsys" program when booted.  Some manual setup is
required before running "newsys".

The disk devices are specified as "/dev/disk#?" where '#' is the SCSI ID of
the disk and '?' is a letter indicating the disk partition.  For destination
disks, use partition 'a'.  For source disks, use the 'c' partition.

Before a Macintosh disk can be accessed by "newsys", it must be formatted and
partitioned with a Macintosh utility such as "HD Setup" or "Silverlining".

Allocate a paging partition, usually 10MB, of type "Apple_Scratch".

Allocate an "Apple_Free" partition for the Unix file system.  This will later
be converted into a "Mach_UNIX_BSD4.3" partition.  For SMALL systems, allocate
at least 60MB.  For FULL systems, allocate at least 100MB.  If SOURCE is
included, add another 300MB.

The best way to install a system that will have sources is to first install
the system without the sources.  Format another disk with a 300MB partition.
Run the "mac2part" program and the "newfs" program.  Mount the 'c' partition
as "/usr/src" and then run "newsys" to update, adding the SOURCE module.

Open the "Mach" control panel.

The "Time Offset" converts between the Macintosh's local time and GMT.  Select
the appropriate offset for your time zone.

The "Default Root Device" is the same as the DESTINATION "Disk Device" that
will be supplied to "newsys".
The "Default Root Device" is where the Mach Kernel finds the Unix Server.

The "Default Load Device" is usually the same as the "Default Root Device".
The "Default Load Device" is where the Mach Kernel is loaded from.

The "Default Kernel" is usually "/mach_kernel".  This is the name of the
file containing the Mach Kernel.

The "Alternate Root Device", "Alternate Load Device" and "Alternate Kernel"
usually reference the "Mach Kernel" that is shipped along with the "Mach"
control panel.  Note that Mach Kernel's can be loaded from Macintosh files.

Check the "Enable Mach startup after restart." box and restart the machine.
The "newsys" program will then complete the installation process.

A final note: The single-user box can be selected when the "Mach Kernel" boots.
This will boot a single user shell.  The "Mach Kernel" that is shipped along
with the "Mach" control panel contains a small ramdisk with some utilities on
it.

